<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="nagata-subgroups">
  <title>Finite subgroups and maximal constants on surfaces</title>
  <section xml:id="surface-triangle-multiplicity">
    <title>Multiplicity in a triangle</title>
    <p>For a bounded real set, its vertical range is the length of its projection to the second coordinate axis. The following estimate is <xref ref="bib-openai-surface"/>, Lemma 4.1.</p>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-surface-triangle-multiplicity">
      <statement><p>Let <m>\rho&gt;0</m>, and let <m>\Delta\subset\mathbb R^2</m> be a triangle of area <m>\rho^2/2</m> and vertical range <m>\rho</m>. Every nonzero <m>F\in\C[U^{\pm1},Z^{\pm1}]</m> supported in <m>\Delta</m> satisfies <m>\ord_{(1,1)}F\leq\rho</m>.</p></statement>
      <proof>
        <p>Let <m>\ell(y)</m> be the horizontal slice length at height <m>y</m>, and let <m>y_0\leq y_1\leq y_2</m> be the vertex heights. If the heights are distinct, <m>\ell</m> increases linearly from zero at <m>y_0</m> to its maximum at <m>y_1</m>, then decreases linearly to zero at <m>y_2</m>. If two heights coincide, there is one linear segment. In both cases the area is one half the vertical range times the greatest slice length. Thus this greatest length is <m>\rho</m>. For <m>0\leq e\leq\rho</m>, the heights with <m>\ell(y)\geq e</m> form an interval of length <m>\rho-e</m>. When the heights are distinct, its endpoints are<md number="no">y_0+\frac e\rho(y_1-y_0),\qquad y_2-\frac e\rho(y_2-y_1).</md>The same formula applies when adjacent heights coincide.</p>
        <p>Write <m>F=\sum_qf_q(U)Z^q</m> with each displayed row nonzero, and let <m>e</m> be the least order of these rows at <m>U=1</m>. Factor each row as <m>f_q=(U-1)^eg_q</m> and let <m>G=\sum_qg_q(U)Z^q</m>. Then <m>F=(U-1)^eG</m> and <m>G(1,Z)\ne0</m>. The factors <m>g_q</m> remain Laurent polynomials. The horizontal exponent span of <m>f_q</m> is exactly <m>e</m> plus that of <m>g_q</m>, because the two extreme coefficients in the product are nonzero. Therefore <m>\ell(q)\geq e</m> for every row, and the heights in <m>G</m> span an interval of length at most <m>\rho-e</m>.</p>
        <p>Specialization to <m>U=1</m> can remove rows but cannot add a height. A power of <m>Z</m>, which is a unit at <m>Z=1</m>, turns <m>G(1,Z)</m> into a polynomial of degree at most <m>\rho-e</m>. Hence<md number="no">\ord_{(1,1)}G\leq\ord_1G(1,Z)\leq\rho-e.</md>The first inequality follows because restriction cannot produce a term of smaller total degree in the Taylor series. Orders add under multiplication of nonzero germs, giving <m>\ord_{(1,1)}F\leq\rho</m>. The orders and exponent spans are integers; the vertices and <m>\rho</m> need not be integral.</p>
      </proof>
    </lemma>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="surface-finite-subgroup">
    <title>A subgroup of prescribed size</title>
    <p>Continue to write <m>\mathbb T=(\C^\times)^2</m> and <m>\mathcal M(\Omega)=\mathcal M(\Omega\cap\Z^2)</m> for a bounded real diagram. For <m>u,\xi\in\mathbb R^2</m>, write <m>\det(u,\xi)</m> for their oriented determinant. An integer vector is primitive if its coordinates are coprime.</p>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-surface-subgroup-test">
      <statement><p>Let <m>V&gt;0</m>, let <m>r</m> be a positive integer, and let <m>P\subset\mathbb R^2</m> be a triangle of area <m>V/2</m>. Suppose that a primitive <m>u\in\Z^2</m> satisfies<md number="no">\max_{\xi\in P}\det(u,\xi)-\min_{\xi\in P}\det(u,\xi)=\sqrt{rV}.</md>There is a subgroup <m>G\subset\mathbb T</m> with exactly <m>r</m> points such that, for all positive integers <m>N,m</m> with <m>m&gt;N\sqrt{V/r}</m>, the <m>J^{m-1}</m> evaluation on <m>\mathcal M(NP)</m> at <m>G</m> is injective.</p></statement>
      <proof>
        <p>Complete <m>u</m> to an integer basis <m>(u,v_0)</m> with <m>\det(u,v_0)=1</m>. Let <m>v=rv_0</m> and <m>M=\Z u+\Z v</m>, a sublattice of index <m>r</m>. For <m>\eta=(\eta_1,\eta_2)</m>, write <m>\chi_\eta(x,z)=x^{\eta_1}z^{\eta_2}</m>. Define<md number="no">G=\{p\in\mathbb T:\chi_\eta(p)=1\text{ for every }\eta\in M\}.</md>The characters <m>X=\chi_u</m> and <m>Y=\chi_{v_0}</m> are torus coordinates because their exponent matrix is unimodular. In these coordinates, <m>G=\{X=1,\ Y^r=1\}</m>, which has exactly <m>r</m> distinct complex points.</p>
        <p xml:id="anchor-subgroup-characteristic">Suppose that <m>0\ne f\in\mathcal M(NP)</m> vanishes to order at least <m>m</m> at all points of <m>G</m>. For <m>g\in G</m>, let <m>(\tau_gf)(p)=f(gp)</m>. Translation permutes <m>G</m> and preserves local orders. For a coset <m>\eta+M</m>, the sum<md number="no">f_\eta=\frac1r\sum_{g\in G}\chi_\eta(g)^{-1}\tau_gf</md>retains precisely the monomials in that coset. Indeed, write <m>\xi-\eta=nu+\ell v_0</m>. Summation over the <m>r</m>th roots of unity gives<md number="no">\frac1r\sum_{g\in G}\chi_{\xi-\eta}(g)=\begin{cases}1,&amp;\xi-\eta\in M,\\0,&amp;\xi-\eta\notin M.\end{cases}</md>Every <m>f_\eta</m> has the prescribed orders and remains supported in <m>NP</m>. At least one component is nonzero.</p>
      <aside xml:id="margin-subgroup-characteristic"><p>This proof uses the <m>r</m> distinct roots of unity and the scalar <m>1/r</m>. If the characteristic divides <m>r</m>, these steps fail and <m>Y\mapsto Y^r</m> is not étale. The later proof instead uses polynomial interpolation at arbitrary distinct points.</p></aside>
        <p>Multiply a nonzero component by the unit <m>\chi_{-\eta}</m>. Its support is in <m>M</m>. In coordinates <m>U=\chi_u</m> and <m>Z=\chi_v=Y^r</m>, there is a nonzero Laurent polynomial <m>F</m> such that<md number="no">\chi_{-\eta}f_\eta=F(U,Z).</md>The map is <m>(X,Y)\mapsto(X,Y^r)</m>, with derivative <m>\operatorname{diag}(1,r)</m> at the identity. It is locally biholomorphic there, and hence <m>\ord_{(1,1)}F\geq m</m>.</p>
        <p>Let <m>A</m> be the real matrix with columns <m>u,v</m>. The support of <m>F</m> is contained in <m>\Delta=A^{-1}(NP-\eta)</m>. Since <m>\det A=r</m>, its area is <m>N^2V/(2r)</m>. The second coordinate of <m>A^{-1}\xi</m> is <m>\det(u,\xi)/r</m>, so its vertical range is <m>N\sqrt{V/r}</m>. Translation by <m>\eta</m> does not change either value. Apply <xref ref="lem-surface-triangle-multiplicity"/> with <m>\rho=N\sqrt{V/r}</m>. It gives <m>\ord_{(1,1)}F\leq N\sqrt{V/r}&lt;m</m>, a contradiction.</p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>This is <xref ref="bib-openai-surface"/>, Proposition 4.2.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="surface-primitive-direction">
    <title>A direction for every large point count</title>
    <p>Return to the fixed surface data <m>D,V,T</m> and the triangles <m>P_t</m> of <xref ref="surface-slice-compression"/>. The freedom in <m>t</m> gives the determinant range required in <xref ref="prop-surface-subgroup-test"/>.</p>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-surface-primitive-direction">
      <statement><p>For every sufficiently large integer <m>r</m>, there are <m>t&gt;T</m> and a primitive <m>u\in\Z^2</m> such that the determinant range of <m>P_t</m> is <m>\sqrt{rV}</m>. The threshold depends only on <m>D,V,T</m>.</p></statement>
      <proof>
        <p>Let <m>W=DV</m>, <m>a_0=WT/(1+T)</m>, and <m>R=\sqrt{rV}</m>. As <m>t</m> varies in <m>(T,\infty)</m>, <m>a=Wt/(1+t)</m> varies continuously and bijectively in <m>(a_0,W)</m>. For sufficiently large <m>R</m>, choose <m>j</m> to be the largest power of two at most <m>R/(2W)</m>. Then<md number="no">\frac{R}{4W}&lt;j\leq\frac{R}{2W},\qquad jW&lt;R.</md>The open interval<md number="no">I_r=\bigl(D(R-jW),\ D(R-ja_0)\bigr)</md>has length <m>Dj(W-a_0)&gt;DR/[4(1+T)]</m>, which exceeds two for all sufficiently large <m>r</m>. It contains an odd integer <m>i&gt;0</m>. Since <m>j</m> is a power of two, <m>u=(i,j)</m> is primitive.</p>
        <p>The strict inequalities defining <m>I_r</m> give<md number="no">a_*=\frac{R-i/D}{j}\in(a_0,W).</md>Choose the unique <m>t&gt;T</m> for which <m>a=a_*</m>. With <m>b=W-a</m>, one has <m>i/D+ja=R</m> and <m>i/D-jb=R-jW&gt;0</m>. The determinant values at the vertices <m>(-b,0)</m>, <m>(a,0)</m>, and <m>(0,1/D)</m> are <m>jb</m>, <m>-ja</m>, and <m>i/D</m>. Their minimum is <m>-ja</m> and maximum is <m>i/D</m>, giving range <m>R</m>. All restrictions on <m>r</m> depend only on the fixed data.</p>
      </proof>
    </lemma>
    <corollary xml:id="cor-surface-uniform-torus-tests">
      <statement><p>There is an integer <m>r_0</m> such that, for each <m>r\geq r_0</m>, one can choose one <m>t&gt;T</m> and one subgroup <m>G\subset\mathbb T</m> with <m>r</m> points for which the <m>J^{m-1}</m> evaluation on <m>\mathcal M(NP_t)</m> is injective for every pair of positive integers <m>N,m</m> satisfying <m>m&gt;N\sqrt{V/r}</m>.</p></statement>
      <proof><p>For fixed <m>r\geq r_0</m>, choose <m>t,u</m> by <xref ref="lem-surface-primitive-direction"/> and apply <xref ref="prop-surface-subgroup-test"/>. The choices of <m>t,G</m> precede the choices of <m>N,m</m>.</p></proof>
    </corollary>
    <p>The direction and the corollary are <xref ref="bib-openai-surface"/>, Lemma 4.3 and Corollary 4.4.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="surface-homogeneous-nef">
    <title>Homogeneous vanishing and nefness</title>
    <p>The surface argument converts the injective tests with equal vanishing orders into an inequality for every curve. It uses positive-square divisors, which need not be nef.</p>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-surface-positive-square">
      <statement><p>Let <m>Y</m> be a smooth integral complex projective surface, let <m>A</m> be a nef real divisor class, and let <m>F</m> be a rational Cartier divisor. If <m>F^2&gt;0</m> and <m>A\cdot F&gt;0</m>, then <m>H^0(Y,\cO_Y(qF))\ne0</m> for every sufficiently large positive integer <m>q</m> divisible by the denominators of <m>F</m>.</p></statement>
      <proof>
        <p xml:id="anchor-surface-riemann-roch">For such integers, Serre duality gives<md number="no">h^2(Y,\cO_Y(qF))=h^0(Y,\cO_Y(K_Y-qF)).</md>For large <m>q</m>, <m>A\cdot(K_Y-qF)&lt;0</m>. An effective divisor has nonnegative intersection with <m>A</m>, so this <m>h^2</m> is zero. Surface Riemann–Roch gives<md number="no">h^0(Y,\cO_Y(qF))\geq\chi(Y,\cO_Y(qF))=\frac{q^2F^2-qF\cdot K_Y}{2}+\chi(Y,\cO_Y)&gt;0</md>for sufficiently large <m>q</m>, because <m>F^2&gt;0</m>. Serre duality is <xref ref="bib-stacks"/> (<url href="https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FVV">Tag 0FVV</url>); the surface Riemann–Roch formula is <xref ref="bib-hartshorne" detail="Theorem V.1.6"/>.</p>
      <aside xml:id="margin-surface-riemann-roch"><p>Only <m>h^2</m> must vanish. Then <m>h^0=\chi+h^1\geq\chi</m>, and the positive square gives a positive quadratic term. The auxiliary divisor itself need not be nef; nefness of <m>A</m> and <m>A\cdot F&gt;0</m> exclude sections of <m>K_Y-qF</m>.</p></aside>
      </proof>
    </lemma>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-surface-homogeneous-nef">
      <statement><p>Let <m>p_1,\ldots,p_r</m> be distinct points of <m>S</m>, set <m>w=\sqrt{V/r}</m>, and suppose that, for all positive integers <m>N,m</m> with <m>m&gt;Nw</m>, no nonzero section of <m>NL</m> vanishes to order at least <m>m</m> at all these points. On their blow-up <m>\pi:Y\to S</m>, with exceptional curves <m>E_i</m>, the class<md number="no">D_0=\pi^*L-w\sum_{i=1}^rE_i</md>is nef.</p></statement>
      <proof>
        <p>Choose a Cartier divisor representing <m>L</m> and write <m>A=\pi^*L</m> and <m>E=\sum_iE_i</m>. The class <m>A</m> is nef. The blowup intersection identities <m>A^2=V</m>, <m>A\cdot E_i=0</m>, and <m>E_i\cdot E_j=-\delta_{ij}</m> give <m>D_0^2=0</m>. If <m>D_0</m> is not nef, there is an integral curve <m>C</m> with <m>D_0\cdot C&lt;0</m>. Since <m>D_0\cdot E_i=w&gt;0</m>, <m>C</m> is not exceptional, and <m>A\cdot C&gt;0</m>.</p>
        <p>For a sufficiently small positive rational <m>\delta</m>,<md number="no">(D_0-\delta C)^2=-2\delta D_0\cdot C+\delta^2C^2&gt;0</md>and <m>A\cdot(D_0-\delta C)=V-\delta A\cdot C&gt;0</m>. Fix such <m>\delta</m>. Continuity gives a rational number <m>s&gt;w</m>, close enough to <m>w</m>, such that<md number="no">F=A-sE-\delta C</md>satisfies <m>F^2&gt;0</m> and <m>A\cdot F&gt;0</m>.</p>
        <p>Choose a sufficiently large positive integer <m>N</m> divisible by the denominators of <m>s</m> and <m>\delta</m>. By <xref ref="lem-surface-positive-square"/>, there is an effective divisor <m>R\sim NF</m>. Since <m>N\delta</m> and <m>m=Ns</m> are positive integers,<md number="no">R+N\delta C\sim NA-mE</md>is effective. For the blow-up of the distinct smooth points,<md number="no">\pi_*\cO_Y(-mE)=\bigcap_{i=1}^r\mathcal I_{p_i}^{\,m}.</md>The projection formula gives a nonzero section of <m>NL</m> vanishing to order at least <m>m</m> at every <m>p_i</m>. This contradicts <m>m=Ns&gt;Nw</m>. The local ideal identity for this surface blowup is <xref ref="bib-stacks"/> (<url href="https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AGS">Tag 0AGS</url>), part (4).</p>
        <p>The displayed blow-up identity expresses the local multiplicity condition: a section of a pullback line bundle descends across the missing points because <m>S</m> is normal, and the exceptional order of a local equation is its ordinary multiplicity. Thus its order along each <m>E_i</m> is precisely membership in <m>\mathcal I_{p_i}^{\,m}</m>.</p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>The positive-square argument and the nefness criterion are <xref ref="bib-openai-surface"/>, Lemma 5.2 and Proposition 5.3.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="surface-completion">
    <title>Maximality at very general tuples</title>
    <p>Fix the nodal data from <xref ref="lem-surface-node"/> and let <m>r_0</m> be the threshold in <xref ref="cor-surface-uniform-torus-tests"/>. For every <m>r\geq r_0</m>, that corollary gives injective torus jets for all positive integers <m>N,m</m> with <m>m&gt;N\sqrt{V/r}</m>. By <xref ref="cor-surface-triangle-transfer"/>, the evaluation on <m>H^0(S,NL)</m> is injective at some tuple of <m>r</m> distinct surface points for each such pair.</p>
    <p>Let <m>U_r(S)</m> be the configuration space of distinct ordered tuples. Each injectivity condition defines a nonempty Zariski-open subset by <xref ref="lem-jets-open"/>. There are countably many pairs <m>N,m</m>, so the conditions hold together outside a countable union <m>Z_r</m> of proper closed subsets of <m>U_r(S)</m>, with nonempty complement. At every tuple in this complement, <xref ref="prop-surface-homogeneous-nef"/> gives nefness of<md number="no">\pi^*L-\sqrt{\frac Vr}\sum_iE_i.</md>The volume bound <xref ref="lem-jets-volume"/> gives the reverse inequality for the Seshadri constant. Therefore<md number="no">\varepsilon(S,L;p_1,\ldots,p_r)=\sqrt{\frac Vr}.</md>This proves the surface case of <xref ref="thm-seshadri-complex"/>, namely <xref ref="bib-openai-surface"/>, Theorem 1.1. The threshold depends only on <m>S,L</m>; it was fixed before the section degree and vanishing order.</p>
  </section>
</chapter>
